---
import CaseStudy from "../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro";

const facts = [
  { k: "Role", v: "Community Operations Specialist" },
  { k: "Focus", v: "Account access, support to product" },
  { k: "Scope", v: "9 NA + 3 Arabic queues, ~145k tickets a month" },
  { k: "Year", v: "2017 · Full-time" },
];
---

<CaseStudy
  title="Surfacing insights on hacked accounts · Cat Tran"
  description="A root-cause analysis of why restored Instagram accounts kept getting re-hacked, and the fixes that broke the loop."
  client="Instagram"
  heading="Surfacing insights on hacked accounts"
  lede="At Instagram I funneled patterns from the support queue into the right product teams. My area was account access. One pattern stood out: accounts we restored kept getting hacked again within days. I ran the root-cause analysis that found out why, and recommended the change that closed the loop."
  facts={facts}
>
  <section>
    <h2 class="eyebrow">The pattern</h2>
    <p>
      Support restored hacked accounts by resetting the password and sending the
      person back in through a recovery checkpoint. It worked, until it didn't. A
      real share of those people wrote back in a few days later, hacked again.
      Restoring the same account two or three times was common enough to look
      like a broken process rather than bad luck.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="eyebrow">The investigation</h2>
    <p>
      Nobody had asked me to look into it, but the repeats bothered me. I pulled
      every account that had reported a hack more than once and walked their
      action histories one by one. The tell was consistent: soon after we
      restored an account, a password reset went out to the email address on
      file.
    </p>
    <p>
      Back in SQL, I charted password resets over time. They spiked hard once a
      week, always around the same hour. That is not what people do. It looked
      automated.
    </p>
    <p>
      I built a dashboard to show the shape and scale of it and took it to the
      Instagram product review group. By early 2017, automated scripts were
      compromising more than 260,000 accounts a week through this gap, then using
      them to post spam and nudity.
    </p>
    <figure class="shot">
      <svg
        class="diagram"
        viewBox="0 0 680 286"
        role="img"
        aria-labelledby="chart-t chart-d"
      >
        <title id="chart-t">Weekly password-reset volume</title>
        <desc id="chart-d">
          A line chart with a low, flat baseline of password resets broken by six
          sharp spikes, one exactly every seven days at the same hour.
        </desc>
        <line x1="46" y1="40" x2="46" y2="234" stroke="#E6DDCC" stroke-width="1"
        ></line>
        <line x1="46" y1="234" x2="654" y2="234" stroke="#E6DDCC"
        stroke-width="1"></line>
        <path
          d="M46,234 L46,231 63,232 70,58 77,229 170,231 180,58 187,228 280,232 290,58 297,230 390,231 400,58 407,229 500,232 510,58 517,230 610,231 620,58 627,230 654,231 L654,234 Z"
          fill="#CB5E3C"
          fill-opacity="0.08"
          stroke="none"></path>
        <polyline
          points="46,231 63,232 70,58 77,229 170,231 180,58 187,228 280,232 290,58 297,230 390,231 400,58 407,229 500,232 510,58 517,230 610,231 620,58 627,230 654,231"
          fill="none"
          stroke="#CB5E3C"
          stroke-width="2"
          stroke-linejoin="round"
          stroke-linecap="round"></polyline>
        <line x1="180" y1="46" x2="290" y2="46" stroke="#6F6252"
        stroke-width="1"></line>
        <line x1="180" y1="46" x2="180" y2="52" stroke="#6F6252"
        stroke-width="1"></line>
        <line x1="290" y1="46" x2="290" y2="52" stroke="#6F6252"
        stroke-width="1"></line>
        <text x="235" y="40" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="#6F6252"
          >7 days</text
        >
        <text x="46" y="24" font-size="12" fill="#6F6252">
          Password reset volume
        </text>
        {
          [70, 180, 290, 400, 510, 620].map((x, i) => (
            <text
              x={x}
              y="252"
              text-anchor="middle"
              font-size="11"
              fill="#6F6252"
            >
              {i + 1}
            </text>
          ))
        }
        <text x="350" y="272" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="#6F6252">
          consecutive weeks
        </text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption>
        A recreation of the dashboard I built to show how automated the resets
        were.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="eyebrow">The loop</h2>
    <p>
      The recovery checkpoint only re-secured one thing: the password. If the
      attacker also controlled the person's email, which was common right after a
      large credential breach somewhere else on the internet, they still had
      everything they needed. A script would request a password reset from the
      email, walk through it, and change the password back. The person would
      write in, we would restore the account, and the cycle started over. Every
      wave of automated resets reached the queue a day or two later as a flood of
      new hacked-account tickets.
    </p>
    <p>
      The only real fix was to move the account onto an email address the
      attacker did not control. The checkpoint never asked for that.
    </p>
    <figure class="shot">
      <svg
        class="diagram"
        viewBox="0 0 680 300"
        role="img"
        aria-labelledby="loop-t loop-d"
      >
        <title id="loop-t">The hacked-account loop</title>
        <desc id="loop-d">
          A four-step cycle: an account gets hacked, the person contacts support,
          support resets the password, the attacker resets it again from the
          hacked email, and the account is hacked once more.
        </desc>
        <defs>
          <marker
            id="ig-arrow"
            viewBox="0 0 10 10"
            refX="8"
            refY="5"
            markerWidth="6.5"
            markerHeight="6.5"
            orient="auto-start-reverse"
          >
            <path
              d="M2 1L8 5L2 9"
              fill="none"
              stroke="#CB5E3C"
              stroke-width="1.6"
              stroke-linecap="round"
              stroke-linejoin="round"></path>
          </marker>
        </defs>

        <g fill="#F8F4ED" stroke="#2A2724" stroke-width="1.25">
          <rect x="25" y="44" width="185" height="50" rx="9"></rect>
          <rect x="247" y="44" width="185" height="50" rx="9"></rect>
          <rect x="469" y="44" width="185" height="50" rx="9"></rect>
          <rect x="180" y="232" width="320" height="50" rx="9"></rect>
        </g>

        <g font-size="12" fill="#2A2724" text-anchor="middle">
          <text x="117" y="73">
            <tspan fill="#CB5E3C" font-weight="600">1</tspan> Account gets hacked
          </text>
          <text x="339" y="73">
            <tspan fill="#CB5E3C" font-weight="600">2</tspan> Person contacts support
          </text>
          <text x="561" y="73">
            <tspan fill="#CB5E3C" font-weight="600">3</tspan> Support resets the password
          </text>
          <text x="340" y="261">
            <tspan fill="#CB5E3C" font-weight="600">4</tspan> Attacker resets it from
            the hacked email
          </text>
        </g>

        <g fill="none" stroke="#CB5E3C" stroke-width="1.75">
          <path d="M210,69 L243,69" marker-end="url(#ig-arrow)"></path>
          <path d="M432,69 L465,69" marker-end="url(#ig-arrow)"></path>
          <path
            d="M561,94 C561,165 520,232 503,244"
            marker-end="url(#ig-arrow)"></path>
          <path
            d="M180,244 C100,232 66,165 100,96"
            marker-end="url(#ig-arrow)"></path>
        </g>
      </svg>
      <figcaption>
        Restore, re-compromise via email, repeat. Recreated for this case study.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="eyebrow">The recommendation</h2>
    <p>
      I took the analysis to the product team with a recommendation: add a
      captcha to the password-reset request, so an automated script could not
      walk through it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Growth pushed back. Adding friction to password resets was not worth the
      cost: legitimate resets vastly outnumber malicious ones, and a captcha
      would slow down real users far more than it stopped scripts.
    </p>
    <p>
      The team converged on a different fix instead: build a secure-email flow
      into the hacked lock, so recovery moves the account onto an email the
      attacker does not control rather than adding friction to every reset. That
      change was prioritized for the start of 2017. The same work fed into the
      two-factor rollout I helped ship soon after.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="eyebrow">Impact</h2>
    <p>
      The pattern tracked external credential breaches. A leaked email-and-password
      pair often unlocked both accounts at once, so every large breach elsewhere
      sent a wave of these cases to the queue. The dashboard made the scale
      legible for the first time.
    </p>
    <ul class="stats">
      <li>
        <span class="stat-v">260k+</span>
        <span class="stat-k">
          accounts a week taken over by password-reset scripts, the scale the
          dashboard surfaced
        </span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="stat-v">Declined</span>
        <span class="stat-k">
          a captcha on password resets, Growth's call: the friction cost more
          than it blocked
        </span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="stat-v">Q1 2017</span>
        <span class="stat-k">
          a secure-email flow for the hacked lock, prioritized on the roadmap
          instead
        </span>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="eyebrow">The wider account-access work</h2>
    <p>
      The re-hacking loop was one thread. I owned the North America and Arabic
      account-access queues and pushed fixes across the surface.
    </p>
    <ul class="hard">
      <li>
        <strong>Account-change emails.</strong> The notifications were spread
        across formats and servers, and their design made accidental disavows
        easy. I made the case for consolidating them onto one server and rewrote
        the templates, cutting 5,500 tickets a month and up to 38 hours of rep
        work.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Tiered support for sensitive accounts.</strong> After a run of
        reps mistakenly granting access to high-profile accounts, I
        project-managed a specialist queue for PR-sensitive requests: the go-live
        plan, the workflow, the training. Volume dropped over 90% and rep-caused
        escalations went from more than ten a half to one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Two-factor authentication.</strong> I led the North America
        rollout of 2FA and in-app login support to 100% of users and pulled the
        impact data. The in-app form, which blocks requests for accounts a
        device has never logged into, took global rep accuracy from 92% to
        around 97%, halved identifiable fake requests, and cut North America
        handling time by 12%. 2FA added 1.1 million users.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="eyebrow">What I took from it</h2>
    <p>
      This is the case for putting an operations person close to product. The
      pattern was sitting in the queue the whole time. Nobody had connected the
      repeat reports to a root cause, and once the data was on the table the
      product teams moved on it fast.
    </p>
  </section>
</CaseStudy>
